<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phra Bang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pha Bang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Phra Bang is a gilded standing Buddha image, the palladium of the Lao monarchy, after which the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> is named. Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum</a> brought it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> in the 14th century together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>. Twice carried off to Siam, it was returned in 1867 and is kept in the former royal palace at Luang Prabang.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phra-bang.jpg" alt="Phra Bang" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tmd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phra_Bang_Budhhalawan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
